## 1. Introduction
Worldwide antibiotic drugs are being widely used for the treatment of bacterial infections, where the high demand for such drugs has seen their global production increase significantly [1]. For example, pefloxacin is one of the most important antibiotics, commonly used in human and veterinary medicines, because it can kill a diverse range of pathogenic bacterial species [2,3]. However, overuse of antibiotics is becoming a major problem. This is because antibiotics are very easily discharged into aquatic ecosystems through wastewater, animal excrement and soil erosion, which have seriously threatened both human and ecological health [4,5]. For example, concerns over the rise in antibiotic resistance have steadily increased in recent years, where the problems associated with the misuse of antibiotics have gained tremendous scientific and public attention [6,7]. Therefore, it is crucial to quickly develop effective techniques to eliminate antibiotics from aquatic environments.
Among the methods that successfully used for removing antibiotics from aqueous solution, adsorption is perhaps one of the most studied methods due to its low-cost and ease of operation [8]. To date, various adsorbents have been developed for the removal of antibiotics from aqueous solutions, including nanoporous carbon [9], zeolite [10], biochar [11] and hydrogels [12]. However, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), a class of crystalline materials, composed of metal ions/clusters and organic ligands [13] have not been widely considered as antibiotic adsorbents. Owing to their ultrahigh porosity, large surface area and easy functionalization, MOFs are increasing proposed as promising adsorbents for antibiotic removal. For example, Xiong et al. [14] reported a new carbon nanotube loaded iron MOF, which had an adsorption capacity of 364, 326 and $181~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for tetracycline hydrochloride, oxytetracycline hydrochloride and chlortetracycline hydrochloride, respectively. Likewise, Wu et al. [15] successfully used a magnetic copper-based MOF for the adsorptive removal of antibiotics from aqueous solutions with adsorption capacities as high as 538 and $513~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ for ciprofloxacin and norfloxacin, respectively. However, although MOF based adsorption has been demonstrated as a simple and effective method for the removal of antibiotics, it has the limitation that it only transfers or concentrates antibiotics on the adsorbate, and does not actually degrade them.
Recently, the integration of adsorption and advanced oxidation for the degradation of organic pollutants in aqueous solutions has attracted much attention [16,17]. Since this integrated technology combines the advantages of both adsorption and advanced oxidation, it has great potential. Specifically, high adsorption is advantageous for promoting the mass transfer of organic pollutants to the surface of materials used for advanced oxidation. Whereas, at the material’s surface, advanced oxidation can realize the degradation of organic pollutants that have been adsorbed. For example, when Li et al. [18] investigated a magnetic $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/C e O_{2}}$ composite for the removal of organic dyes, the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/C e O_{2}}$ composite synergistically played a dual-role as both adsorbent and Fenton-like catalyst. In addition, Wang et al. [19] found that Fe-based MOFs could be used simultaneously for adsorption and photocatalytic degradation of tetracycline. Moreover, Min et al. [20] found that heterostructured $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{HKUST}-1$ photocatalysts, when used for the removal of methylene blue, had a degradation process involving integration of adsorption and visible light photocatalysis. Therefore, given that recent studies have revealed that Fe and Fe-based MOFs are promising catalysts for the removal of organic contaminants [21–23], and MOFs have also been shown to be excellent adsorbents for the removal of antibiotics, a combination of these two approaches should enhance removal efficiencies. However, while $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B has been successfully used for the removal of organic contaminants by either adsorption or Fenton oxidation, these approaches have never been applied together. To address this research gap, functional $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B having both adsorption and Fenton oxidation capacity should be prepared and evaluated. However, there is a considerable knowledge gap in the current literature, because it is relatively challenging to develop $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B capable of simultaneous adsorption and Fenton oxidation for specific target organic contaminants. Thus, in this study, the key knowledge gaps to be addressed were (1) the relative importance of adsorption and Fenton oxidation by $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B, (2) the factors affecting the removal efficiency of pefloxacin, and (3) the removal mechanism of pefloxacin by $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B.
Hence, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B was used for the removal of pefloxacin from aqueous solution and SEM/EDS, FTIR, and XRD were used to monitor surface’s changes in $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B before and after pefloxacin removal. The pefloxacin degradation efficiencies were also evaluated under various operational conditions. Finally, a detailed mechanism for pefloxacin degradation by $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B was proposed based on kinetic studies, as well as analysis of degradation products by liquid chromatography mass spectrometry (LC-MS).
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Chemicals
2-aminoterephthalic acid $(\mathsf{C}_{8}\mathsf{H}_{7}\mathsf{N O}_{4}$ , $98\%$ , Poly (ethylene glyco)-biock-poly (Propylene glycol)-block-poly (thylene glycol) was purchased from Aladdin Industrial Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). N, N-dimethyl formamide $(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{7}\mathsf{N O},\mathsf{99}.5\%)$ , Ferric chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $99.0\%$ , were obtained from Xi Long Scientific Co., Ltd, China. Chromatographic grade acetonitrile $(C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{N}$ $99.9\%$ was purchased from Guangdong Guanghua Sci-Tech Co., Ltd, China. Formic acid $\mathrm{\CH}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ , $98\%$ ) was obtained from Fu Chen Chemical Reagent Factory, China. Pefloxacin $(\mathsf{C}_{17}\mathrm{H}_{20}\mathsf{F N}_{3}0_{3}$ , $98\%$ ) and hydrogen peroxide solution $\mathrm{(H}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ , $30\%)$ were purchased from Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). All chemicals were used as supplied without further purification and all solutions were prepared in deionized water.
## 2.2. Synthesis of $N H_{2}$ -MIL-88B
$\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B was prepared as described previously with slight modification [24]. Initially, poly (ethylene glycol)-block-poly (propylene glycol)-block-poly (ethylene glycol) $(0.56~\mathrm{g})$ , ${\mathrm{FeCl}}_{3}{\cdot}6{\mathrm{H}}_{2}.$ - O $(0.324\mathrm{~g},2\mathrm{~mmol})$ and acetic acid $(0.5~\mathrm{mL}$ ) were dissolved in deionized water $\mathrm{:}15\mathrm{\mL}$ . Thereafter, 2-aminoterephthalic acid $(0.3623\ \mathrm{g},2\ \mathrm{mmol}$ ) was dissolved in DMF $\mathrm{12~mL}$ ). The two separate solutions were then mixed together at room temperature and transferred into a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ -Teflon autoclave for hydrothermal treatment at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{24\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The product obtained was collected by filtration through a $0.22\upmu\mathrm{m}$ membrane and washed with water and ethanol three times before being dried overnight at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The final solid product obtained was stored in a glass desiccator.
## 2.3. Characterization and analysis
The concentration of pefloxacin in solution was quantified by high performance liquid chromatography (HPLC, Agilent 1260, USA) and coupled with mass spectrometry to characterize the target antibiotic degradation products. Acetonitrile and $0.1\%$ formic acid solutions were used as the mobile phase, with a flow rate of $1\ \mathrm{mL}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , a column temperature of $35\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ a detection wavelength of 271 and $287~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , and an injection volume of $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ . The crystal phase structures of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B before and after reaction were examined by XRD (Bruker D8 Focus Powder XRD, Germany) where 2h was scanned from 5 to $50^{\circ}$ at a scanning rate of $3^{\circ}$ per min. The infrared spectrum of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B was measured by FTIR (Thermo Scientific Nicolet Is 10, USA). Changes in morphology of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B were observed by SEM (Hitachi S-4800, Japan).
## 2.4. Batch experiments
Batch experiments were performed to investigate the effects of operational parameters on $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B based Fenton-like reactions. Batch experiments were performed in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ brown glass centrifuge tubes, where a known amount of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B powder and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ were added into pefloxacin solutions $(20~\mathrm{mL}$ ). Thereafter the glass centrifuge tubes were placed in a temperature controlled shaker operating at a 250 rpm and incubated for extended periods. Periodically, samples were removed from the tubes at scheduled intervals and the solutions filtered through $0.45~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ membranes prior to residual pefloxacin analysis by HPLC. The removal efficiency $R\left(\%\right)$ was calculated using:
$$
R(\%)=\frac{(C_{\mathrm{0}}-C_{\mathrm{t}})}{C_{\mathrm{0}}}\times100\
$$
Where $C_{\circ}$ $(\mathrm{mg~L^{-1}})$ is the initial concentration of pefloxacin in solution and $C_{\mathrm{t}}$ $(\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ is the concentration of adsorbate in solution at time t (min).
During kinetic experiments, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B $\left\langle10{\mathrm{~mg}}\right\rangle$ was added into a pefloxacin solution having an initial concentration of $20\mathrm{~mg~L^{-1}}$ . These experiments were carried out at three temperatures (293, 303, and $313\ \mathrm{K}$ ) over $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Fenton-like reactions were also carried out at three temperatures (293, 303, and 313 K) for $1.5\mathrm{h}$ .
## 3. Result and discussion
## 3.1. The removal of pefloxacin by $N H_{2}$ -MIL-88B
When using $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ alone, only a relatively low pefloxacin removal efficiency $(\sim10.0\%)$ was achieved (Fig. 1), which indicated that $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ had a very limited effect on pefloxacin degradation in the absence of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B. Similar results were reported previously, where the low removal efficiency achieved by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was always attributed to weak oxidative degradation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ towards organic contaminants [23,25]. In addition, without $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B alone was three times more efficient, with a pefloxacin removal efficiency of $30.5\%$ . This indicated that, as a typical MOF material, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B had a relatively high adsorption capacity for organic pollutants in aqueous solutions, including pefloxacin [26]. However, complete removal of pefloxacin was achieved within $120\mathrm{min}$ using $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B in combination, suggesting that $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B based Fenton-like oxidation was mainly responsible for the removal of pefloxacin. These high removal efficiencies (Fig. 1) suggested that while $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B can serve as a good adsorbent for pefloxacin removal, the removal efficiency by $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ - MIL-88B only based on adsorption was still significantly less than that observed for $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B based Fenton-like oxidation $(99\%)$ . In addition, it can be reasonably inferred that both adsorption and Fenton-like oxidation should contribute to the total removal of pefloxacin in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B.
## 3.2. Characterization and analysis
HPLC analysis (Fig. 2) of pefloxacin before (Fig. 2a) and after adsorption (Fig. 2b) or Fenton-like oxidation (Fig. 2c) showed that the retention time of pefloxacin was consistently $11.9\ \mathrm{min}$ . After $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B based adsorption, the intensity of the pefloxacin peak was significantly decreased, indicating that a lot of pefloxacin was being removed via an adsorption process. In addition, no new peaks were observed after adsorption alone, which provided further evidence that the removal of pefloxacin by $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B in the absence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was only attributed to $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B based adsorption. However, after exposure to a $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B based Fenton-like process, three new peaks were observed at retention times of 1.8, 7.5 and $9.75\mathrm{min}$ , respectively, suggesting that pefloxacin was degraded by $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B based Fenton-like oxidation. Detailed identification of these degradation products was also performed by LC-MS in the following section of mechanism study.
The XRD patterns of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B before and after adsorption and Fenton-like oxidation (Fig. 3) showed the characteristic peaks of as-synthesized $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B at 2h of 9.42, 10.67, 16.99, 18.71 and $21.32^{\circ}$ , which were in good agreement with values reported in the literatures [24], confirming that $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B was successfully synthesized. After adsorption of pefloxacin the peak at $21.32^{\circ}$ disappeared; only to reappear after Fenton-like oxidation. This potentially indicated a transient change in the crystal shape of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B after the adsorption of pefloxacin, which returned to the original state after Fenton-like oxidative degradation of pefloxacin.
The successful synthesis of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B was also clearly demonstrated by SEM. The as-prepared $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B exhibited a needle-shaped morphology (Fig. 4a) with a length of $\sim1.5\:\mathrm{mm}$ and a diameter of $\sim300\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which agreed very well with the morphology previously reported for $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B [27]. Comparison of SEM images of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B before (Fig. 4a), after adsorption (Fig. 4b) and after Fenton-like oxidation (Fig. 4c), showed that there were some granules before adsorption and aggregates after adsorption which adhered to the surface of the $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B (Fig. 4a and b). Prior to adsorption these granules (Fig. 4a) may simply be residual raw materials from the synthetic process of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B. While the aggregates observed after adsorption (Fig. 4b) were more likely to be indicative of pefloxacin adsorbed on to the surface of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ - MIL-88B. However, both granules and aggregates were significantly reduced after Fenton-like oxidation (Fig. 4c). Moreover, even after Fenton-like oxidation, the SEM images indicated that $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ - MIL-88B still maintained good clear morphology, which indicated that the as-prepared $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B should be reusable. The EDS derived elemental surface concentrations of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B before and after adsorption and Fenton-like oxidation (Fig. 4d-f) showed that fluorine content was $1.51\%$ after adsorption and was reduced to $0.096\%$ after the Fenton-like oxidation, which supported initial adsorption on the surface and subsequent pefloxacin degradation.
The FTIR spectra of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B before and after Fenton-like oxidation are depicted in Fig. 5. Prior to Fenton oxidation, two distinct peaks were observed in the FTIR spectra of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B at 3468 and $3376\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which were caused by symmetric and asymmetric stretching vibrations of the primary amino group, respectively [28]. The absorption peaks were the same as those generated by free 2-aminoterephthalic acid, indicating that the as-prepared $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B contained amino groups. After Fentonlike oxidation the FTIR spectra remained largely unchanged, indicating that the as-prepared $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B had good stability and hence potential for high reusability.
To understand the possible pathways of pefloxacin degradation by $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B based adsorption and Fenton-like oxidation, the degradation products were identified by LC-MS. The results (Fig. 6) showed that several different degradation products were detected and the molecular structures were given in Fig. 7.
It is well known that the hydroxyl radical ( OH) has an extremely strong ability to degrade organic pollutants [29], where essentially all chemical bonds within organic molecules that come into contact with OH can be broken. Fig. 7 shows some of the possible molecular degradation pathways for pefloxacin.
Previous studies have shown that under the action of OH, one degradation pathway led to the production of p1 due to fluorine solvolysis and reductive defluorination [30]. However, this is not the only way to remove fluorine, and alternative pathways in Fig. 7 are also possible when considering the ascending order of bond energies in pefloxacin. For example, initially OH attacked on the carbon–nitrogen single bond (CAN) could lead to the $P2\rightarrow P3\rightarrow P4$ degradation path based on the molecular size of the degradation products, followed by OH attacking on the carbon–nitrogen single bond attached to the benzene ring to produce P5. Finally, followed by carbon–carbon single bond (CAC), carbon– carbon double bond $({\mathsf{C}}{=}{\mathsf{C}})$ , carbon–oxygen double bond $\scriptstyle(=0)$ and ring opening and degradation of the benzene ring $(\mathsf{P5}\to\mathsf{P8},\mathsf{P9},\mathsf{S1}$ ). Thereafter pefloxacin would be finally degraded into water and carbon dioxide. This mechanism is supported by the mass fragments detected by HPLC-MS.
## 3.3. Batch experiments
pH is an important factor that affects both Fenton-like oxidation and adsorption processes [31]. Here the effects of three different pH values from 5.0 to 7.0 were investigated (Fig. 8a). The efficiency of the Fenton-like oxidative degradation of pefloxacin increased as pH decreased because more OH was produced in aqueous solutions under acidic conditions [22].
The initial contaminant concentration was also a key parameter that affected the efficiency of pefloxacin degradation. The effects of three different initial pefloxacin concentrations (10, 20 and $30~\mathrm{mg}$ ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ ) were investigated at $\mathsf{p H}6.0$ and $303\mathrm{K},$ with a fixed concentration of 8 mmol $\mathrm{L}^{-1}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ and a $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B dose of $0.5\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . As shown in Fig. 8b, the removal efficiency was significantly higher when the concentration of pefloxacin was $10\mathrm{~mg~L^{-1}}$ . This was due to a greater number of active sites being available on the $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B surface at the lower pefloxacin concentration [21]. Thereafter, with the increase in pefloxacin concentration, the number of active sites of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B became gradually occupied.
The effect of temperature on the removal efficiency of pefloxacin is shown in Fig. 8c when reacted at $\mathsf{p H}~6.0$ , with an initial pefloxacin concentration of $20~\mathrm{mg~L^{-1}}$ and 8 mmol $\mathrm{L}^{-1}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ , and a $0.5\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B dose. While the changes in temperature had little influence on the pefloxacin removal efficiency, the results (Fig. 8c) did suggest that $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B had a good thermal stability.
The effect of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B dose (0.25, 0.5 and $0.75\mathrm{~g~L~}^{-1}.$ ) on removal efficiency was investigated at a constant initial pefloxacin concentration of $20\mathrm{\mg\L^{-1}}$ . All experiments were performed at $303\mathrm{K}$ and $\mathsf{p H}6.0$ , with 8 mmol $\mathrm{L}^{-1}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The maximum pefloxacin removal efficiency $(100\%)$ was observed when the dose was $0.25\mathrm{~g~}$ ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ . However, at 0.5 and $0.75\mathrm{~g~L~}^{-1}$ , the removal efficiencies only decreased slightly to 95.1 and $94.4\%$ , respectively. While the adsorption efficiency was directly proportional to the $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL88B dose (Fig. 8d), Fenton-like efficiency was inversely proportional to the $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B dose. This was because, while pefloxacin adsorption capacity increased with increasing dose, the corresponding increase in iron ion concentrations also inhibited the efficiency of Fenton-like oxidation [21–23].
Since the amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ present is also a critical factor in Fenton-like oxidation performance, it is often necessary to explore the effect of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration [32]. The initial concentration of pefloxacin used was $20\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , and the $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B dose, temperature, and $\mathsf{p H}$ were constant at $0.5\ \mathrm{g\L^{-1}}$ , $303\ \mathrm{K},$ , and 6.0, respectively. Under these conditions, changes in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration only affected the Fenton-like process, and the removal efficiency increased with an increase in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration (Fig. 8e).Since $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ reacts with $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ in $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B to produce OH for oxidative degradation of pefloxacin, the removal efficiencies reached 92.8 and $100\%$ within $210\ \mathrm{min}$ when the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentrations were 4 and $8\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , respectively. When the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration was $12\mathrm{\mmol\L^{-1}}$ , the degradation efficiency reached $100\%$ in $180~\mathrm{{min}}$ .
## 3.4. Kinetic analysis
## 3.4.1. Adsorption kinetics
In order to further elucidate the adsorption process of pefloxacin by $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B, adsorption kinetics were undertaken and the
data fit to either the pseudo-first-order (equation (2)) or pseudosecond-order (equation (3)) kinetic models:
$$
\ln{(q_{e}-q_{t})}=\ln{q_{e}}-k_{1}t
$$
$$
\frac{t}{q_{t}}=\frac{1}{k_{2}q_{e}^{2}}+\frac{t}{q_{e}}
$$
Where $q_{e}[\mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}]$ and $q_{t}[\mathbf{mg}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}]$ were the adsorption capacities of pefloxacin at equilibrium and at time $t$ (min), respectively; and $k_{1}\mathrm{\:(min^{-1})}$ and $k_{2}$ $\mathsf{\Omega}[\mathrm{g\mg\min}]^{-1}\vert$ were the rate constants of adsorption for the pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order kinetic models, respectively. The kinetic parameters calculated from the best fit of the data to the pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order kinetic models are given in Table 1 for a fixed temperature of $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Since the resulting best correlation coefficients for the pseudo-first-order model ${}^{\prime}\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.771{\cdot}$ were significantly smaller than those of the pseudo-second-order model $\mathbf{\Phi}^{'}\mathbf{R}^{2}=0.974)$ (Table 1) this indicated that the adsorption process at $303\mathrm{~K~}$ was better described by the pseudo-second-order model, which suggested that the adsorption of pefloxacin by $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B was controlled by chemisorption.
## 3.4.2. Oxidation kinetics
To better understand the process of pefloxacin removal, oxidation kinetics of the Fenton-like process were also studied and the data fit to two kinetic models, the pseudo-first-order kinetic model (equation (4)) and the pseudo-second-order kinetic model (equation (5)).
$$
\ln{\frac{C}{C_{0}}}=-k_{o b s}t
$$
$$
\ln(\frac{1}{C_{t}}-\frac{1}{C_{0}})=k t
$$
Where $k_{o b s}$ and $k$ represented the pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order rate constants, respectively; $C_{t}$ was the residual pefloxacin concentration in the solution after the removal for t min and $C_{0}$ was the initial concentration of pefloxacin. As shown in Table 2, the Fenton-like process data fitted well with both the pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order kinetic models. Nevertheless, the pseudo-second-order kinetic model seemed to be slightly more suitable, suggesting that pefloxacin was rapidly oxidized and degraded by a pseudo-second-order oxidation process.
## 3.5. The mechanism based on adsorption and Fenton-like oxidation
On the basis of the aforementioned results, a possible mechanism for the removal of pefloxacin via adsorption and Fentonlike oxidation was proposed. The adsorption kinetic studies indicated that pefloxacin adsorption by $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B was dominated by chemical adsorption. Subsequently, when $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was added after the adsorption had reached equilibrium, the central metal ion in $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B, $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ could then react with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to induce a series of reactions (equations 6–9) [21], which in turn produced a large number of OH that can attack various chemical bonds in the pefloxacin molecules until pefloxacin was completely degraded into carbon dioxide and water. The mechanism for the removal of pefloxacin by $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B based on adsorption and Fentonlike oxidation was proposed, as shown in Fig. 9.
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{Fe}(\Pi\mathrm{I}){+}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}{\longrightarrow}\mathrm{Fe}(\Pi){+}\mathsf{H}\mathsf{O}_{2}{\bullet}{+}\mathsf{H}^{+}}\\ &{}\\ &{\mathrm{Fe}(\Pi){+}\mathsf{H}202{\longrightarrow}\mathrm{Fe}(\Pi\mathrm{I}){+}\bullet\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H}{+}\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H}^{-}}\\ &{}\\ &{\mathrm{Fe}(\Pi\mathrm{I}){+}\mathsf{H}02{\bullet}{\longrightarrow}\mathrm{Fe}(\Pi){+}\mathsf{O}2{+}\mathsf{H}^{+}}\end{array}
$$
Pefloxacin OH $\rightarrow$ CO2 H2O
## 3.6. Reusability of $N H_{2}$ -MIL-88B
The reusability of $N H_{2}{-}M I L{-}88\mathrm{B}$ for the removal of pefloxacin via adsorption and-Fenton-like oxidation was also evaluated (Fig. 10). The initial removal efficiency via adsorption was $46.7\%$ . Thereafter, the removal efficiency decreased with each cycle, being decreased to $16\%$ after the second adsorption cycle, slowly decreasing to $13.9\%$ after 4 cycles. In contrast, the removal efficiency of the first cycle of Fenton-like process was $100\%$ , which was only slightly decreased to $88.2\%$ after 4 cycles. It was thus concluded that $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ - MIL-88B had a good reusability for the removal of pefloxacin via adsorption and-Fenton-like oxidation.
## 4. Conclusion
In this study, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B was successfully synthesized and subsequently used for the removal of pefloxacin from aqueous solutions via adsorption and Fenton-like oxidation. The asprepared $\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{MIL}–88\mathrm{B}$ exhibited a high adsorption capacity for pefloxacin reaching $41.4~\mathrm{mg}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ in $120\ \mathrm{min}$ , where adsorption was dominated by chemical adsorption. Subsequently, a $100\%$ pefloxacin removal efficiency was achieved by $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B based on Fenton-like oxidation, via a pseudo-second-order kinetic model, which suggested that pefloxacin was rapidly oxidized and degraded by a pseudo-second-order oxidation process. Finally, material characterization, analysis of pefloxacin degradation products supported by LC-MS, allowed a potential mechanism for pefloxacin removal by $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B to be proposed based on adsorption and Fenton-like oxidation. Overall, this study demonstrated that $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-88B could be used for integrated adsorption and Fenton-like processes associated with the removal of pefloxacin from aqueous solutions.
